    <!-- Effective per-segment level: forward-fill that SKIPS zero-width segments. A segment is a phantom
         (zero width) when its left x equals the next segments left x (cuw_tcLX{k+1} == cuw_tcLX{k}); this
         happens when dead time shifts an activation edge onto a later edge (e.g. CC0 + line dead time lands
         exactly on CC1), collapsing the level between them to zero time. Such a phantom level is never a real
         run, so it must not split the surrounding equal-level interval into two timings. Carrying the last
         positive-width level across phantoms makes the run-start test below compare GENUINE levels, so a
         cancelled (zero-width) excursion merges its neighbours into one run. With no zero-width segment every
         width is positive and cuw_tcEL{k} == cuw_tcL{k}, so non-cancelled diagrams are byte-identical. -->
