    <!-- ============== Zero-width pulse (0-time peak) suppression for line_out / line_compl_out periods 1..3 ==============
         In dead-time mode an output's ACTIVATING (rising) edge is delayed by its dead time (cuw_st*s/cuw_x*_Os/_Cs
         source-aware running-max chains). If that delayed activation is pushed to (or past) the very next DEACTIVATING edge,
         the pulse's on-time is shorter than the dead time and the part NEVER actually drives the line - no pulse
         appears. The running-max chain collapses the two transition X's onto the same pixel, so the raw polyline
         would still emit an up-then-down vertical stub at that X: a spurious 0-time peak. (Example: Compare0 below
         CounterInit + a large complement dead time produces a 0-width line_compl_out spike at the start of the
         graph even though the real part stays inactive.)
         Fix: a FORWARD-FILL of the per-segment Y level. Each polyline segment k spans [X_k .. X_{k+1}]; when that
         span has zero width (X_{k+1} &lt;= X_k after the running-max clamp) the segment can only ever draw a vertical
         stub, so its level is replaced by the NEXT drawn level (cascaded right-to-left). A genuine pulse whose
         leading segment is zero-width but whose body still has width keeps its active level (the body segment is
         non-zero, so the fill stops there); only a pulse whose ENTIRE width collapsed is flattened, removing the
         spike. Segments with real width are untouched, so fitting / non-dead-time waveforms render unchanged.
         Only periods 1..3 carry the dead-time-shifted, source-aware (_Os/_Cs) running-max vertices that can collapse this way;
         periods 4..8 already use the clipped-tail freeze (cuw_co_*/cuw_cn_*). Period 3's tail fills into the
         period-4 entry level (cuw_co_4_0 / cuw_cn_4_0), exactly what the polyline draws next. Defined here (after
         all X vertices, raw levels and the period-4 entry levels) and consumed only by the waveform substitutions;
         the raw cuw_p*y*/cuw_p*ny* are left intact because cuw_stOs/cuw_stCs reference them (avoids a self-cycle). -->
    <!-- line_out (period 3 first: its tail fills into the period-4 entry level cuw_co_4_0). The collapse test
         uses the SOURCE-AWARE _Os / stOs vertices (exactly what the SVG polyline draws with) so the zero-width
         fill matches the actual geometry for every Output Lines Source; defaults (line_out = PWM) reduce _Os to _O. -->
    <ParamString id="cuw_p3y4d" name="cuw_p3y4d" group="General" default="`${(cuw_b3_Os &lt;= cuw_xD3_Os) ? cuw_co_4_0 : cuw_p3y4}`" visible="false" editable="false" desc="" />
